netdaemon - recovered 1.6 binary and its disassembly

This is the network daemon named in network(4): it is started from /etc/rc,
reads /etc/netmap, and moves the files that netcp leaves in
/usr/spool/net/out.  Its man page and source were not recovered; this
directory holds the binary that was.

	netdaemon.bin	the recovered executable, 19188 bytes
	netact.bin	the recovered request agent, 19002 bytes
	mail16.bin	the recovered mail program, 19169 bytes
	netmail16.bin	the recovered network mail sender, 16754 bytes
	netdaemon.dis	an annotated listing, generated with src/tools/disas
	netdaemon.ctl	the symbols disas used
	ANALYSIS.md	the decompiled flow: main, daemon loop, protocol layer
	netdaemon.c	the reconstruction; `make` cross builds it
	GNUmakefile	makefile

netdaemon.c is a reconstruction, not the original source.  The daemon
loop, spool scans, netmap and ttys handling follow the binary, and the
wire format is the real 1.6 line protocol: three checksum characters
per line, then `P!`/`A!`, `F`/`Y`, the S or R header and its Y, every D
record and its Y, and the unacknowledged E.  Two reconstructed daemons
exchange the same bytes as two recovered ones.  The sender does not
stop for a Y between lines: the 1.6 receiver reads the next record
before it answers the previous one, so F, the header, every D and the
E go as one burst, and the Y answers are drained afterward.  A P! that
arrives during that drain is the R reply and is handled there.

The R path is store-and-forward, and the recovered daemons were run
through it over two hops (a -- b -- c):

- a's netcp queues `R c /etc/motd a /tmp/fetched root`; a's daemon
  routes it to b, b's daemon forwards the same R to c.
- c's daemon writes the inbound record to /usr/spool/net/in/ and execs
  /bin/netact.  netact runs /bin/netcp, which copies /etc/motd into an
  S record for a; c's daemon forwards that S to b, and b forwards it to
  a.  a's daemon execs netact, which copies it to /tmp/fetched.
- A push (`netcp /tmp/hello c%/tmp/hello` on a) runs the same relay in
  one direction; the file arrives on c.

Two things are required and were the earlier failures:
/etc/passwd must carry the `network` user (uid 9), which netact looks
up, and every machine's /etc/netmap must name the return route - c's
netmap says `b a`, or netcp on c reports "No such network address".
netact itself is the request agent: it resolves the inbound record and
runs netcp for a copy (or mail for a message); netact.bin is here for
the three-machine test.

The reconstruction performs the copy in-process instead of exec'ing
netact, so it needs neither netact nor the network user; the wire is
the same.  The 1.6 daemon forks a reader per port, while this one polls
with a one second alarm in a single process.  The end-to-end tests are `nettest16.py recon` (one link),
`nettest16.py relay` (three recovered machines),
`nettest16.py relayrecon` (the reconstruction), and the mixed tests
`nettestmix.py ours-netcp` and `nettestmix.py ours-daemon`, which put
one of our programs at a and the stock chain at b and c.

## Errors

A line whose checksum fails gets a NAK (`N`); the sender resends that
line.  Each D record's sequence is checked, and the E record carries
the next sequence number, so a dropped record is caught at E: the
receiver NAKs, discards its temporary file, and the sender retries the
whole transfer.  The destination is written to `<path>.net` and renamed
only on success, so a failed transfer leaves no half file.

`nettesterr.py` injects a bad checksum on a record, a bad proposal, a
wrong sequence, and a dropped record.  All four recover; the captured
traffic shows the NAK and the retransmission (or the second whole
transfer for the dropped record).

The binary came from Micronix 1.6 Distribution Vol. 3 (/bin/netdaemon),
extracted with readall.  The 1.61 Vol. 3 catalog container carries the same
image.  Header: Whitesmiths object, text 0x44a8 at 0x100, data 0x063c at
0x45a8; entry 0x100, main at 0x02aa.

Calling convention, from the listing:

	cent 0x454d	makes DE the frame pointer, stacking the old one
	cret 0x4554	SP back from DE, pop DE, ret
	arguments	at DE+4 upward, return value in BC

The literal pools are inside the text segment, after a call, not in the
data segment.  disas follows the call into them and reads "netact" as
instructions; netdaemon.dis has a post-pass that turns that first pool
(0x010e..0x0161) into DB.  The .ctl carries only the symbols, so
`disas -f netdaemon.ctl netdaemon.bin` reproduces the listing except for
that pool.

The strings name the protocol's pieces: /bin/netact, /usr/spool/net/in and
/usr/spool/net/out, and the diagnostics "lrec fails", "accept ()",
"sequence error", "collision", "ngets - timeout" and "nopen ()".

The tree's extra/decomp is a C-level experiment and does not model these
inline literals, so the listing is the decompilation here, not C source.

## Mail

The network mail record is an M header:

	M <remote-host> <remote-user> <local-host> <local-user>

then D records and an E, in the same line framing.  It routes by the
first field like an S record.  At the destination, the daemon writes
the record to /usr/spool/net/in/ and execs /bin/netact; netact runs
/bin/mail, which puts the message in /usr/spool/mail/<user>.

mail(1) and netmail are not reconstructed.  mail detects a network name
(machine%user) and execs /bin/netmail; netmail writes the M record into
the outbound spool.  mail16.bin and netmail16.bin are here so the tests
can drive the stock front end.

The reconstruction routes and relays an M record by the same rules as
S, and at the destination writes it to the inbound spool and execs
/bin/netact.  `nettestmail.py dest` (stock sender, our daemon at the
destination) and `nettestmail.py relay` (our daemon between two stock
machines) verify it.
